---
import Guide from "../../../layouts/Guide.astro"
import Code from "../../../components/Code.astro"
import DocLink from "../../../components/DocLink.astro"
import InstallCommand from "./partials/InstallCommand.astro"
import NextSteps from "./partials/NextSteps.astro"
import PickAndChoose from "./partials/PickAndChoose.astro"
---

<Guide overline="Guide">
  <Fragment slot="title">Getting started</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    OPUI ships Solid 2 components, in preview while Solid 2 is a release
    candidate. Install the package, import what you need, and you're set.
  </Fragment>

  <section id="installation">
    <h2>Install via NPM</h2>
    <p>
      The components ship as uncompiled TypeScript (<code>.tsx</code> and
      <code>.ts</code>), so your build has to compile them. They need Solid 2,
      so add <code>solid-js</code> and <code>@solidjs/web</code> if they aren't
      installed yet. Vite with <code>@solidjs/vite-plugin</code> compiles them
      as they are, for server rendering too: it picks up the package through its
      <code>solid</code> export condition. Each component file starts with
      <code>/** @jsxImportSource @solidjs/web */</code>, so TypeScript reads it
      as Solid JSX whatever your own <code>jsxImportSource</code> is.
    </p>
    <InstallCommand />
  </section>

  <section id="css">
    <h2>Import the CSS</h2>
    <p>
      Solid components ship markup only - the CSS still has to be imported once,
      in the entry file that renders your app. Import everything:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`// src/index.tsx
import "opui-css/css/imports.css"`}
      lang="tsx"
    />
    <p>
      Or pick and choose in a CSS file, and import that file in your entry
      instead:
    </p>
    <PickAndChoose file="src/styles/main.css" />
    <Code
      code={`// src/index.tsx
import "./styles/main.css"`}
      lang="tsx"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="usage">
    <h2>How to use</h2>
    <Code
      code={`import { Button } from "opui-css/solid"

export default function App() {
  return <Button variant="filled">Click me</Button>
}`}
      lang="tsx"
    />
    <p>
      Containers take parts, such as <code>CardHeader</code> and
      <code>DialogFooter</code>. Other slots are props: the default slot is
      <code>children</code> and named slots are camelCased, such as
      <code>endText</code>. Form controls take the native <code>value</code> or
      <code>checked</code>, which set the state and follow it when it changes,
      and report changes with <code>onInput</code> and <code>onChange</code>.
      Groups such as <code>ToggleGroup</code> take <code>value</code> and
      <code>onValueChange</code>.
    </p>
    <NextSteps />
  </section>

  <section id="ssr">
    <h2>Server rendering</h2>
    <p>
      Every component renders complete HTML on the server. The native controls
      work and submit with their form. A few things only update on the client:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/range#value">Range</DocLink> value: the
        <code>&lt;output&gt;</code> doesn't follow the thumb. The track fill is
        CSS, so it's fine.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Hydrate those or render them on the client. Component pages say what needs
      hydration next to the browser support chips, and the API tables mark each
      prop with a crossed-out server icon.
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
